{"nodes": [{"id": "benchmark_report_figures_synchronization_chart", "label": "Synchronization Policy Throughput Chart (Frequent synchronization limits MLP throughput)", "file_type": "image", "source_file": "benchmark/report/figures/synchronization.svg", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "description": "Matplotlib grouped bar chart. X: synchronization policy (batch 32, uploaded inputs) with categories Every step, Every 5 steps, End of window. Y: training samples / second (0-5000). Series: Eager (blue) vs Capture (teal). Bars use total synchronized window time; dots show each of 3 repeats. Approx bar values - Eager: every step ~983, every 5 steps ~5061, end of window ~3414; Capture: every step ~1435, every 5 steps ~3377, end of window ~4580. Repeat dots - Eager every step ~856/1063/1060, every 5 ~4932/4932/5342, end ~4697/4370/2289; Capture every step ~1098/1594/1806, every 5 ~3633/3871/2818, end ~5679/4970/3600.", "rationale": "Per-step synchronization drops throughput to ~1000-1400 samples/s for both modes, while syncing every 5 steps or only at end of window gives 3-5x higher throughput. The ordering between Eager and Capture is nonmonotonic across policies, which the caption attributes to timing variability rather than a real effect."}, {"id": "benchmark_report_figures_synchronization_training_samples_per_second", "label": "Training samples / second", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "benchmark/report/figures/synchronization.svg", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "benchmark_report_figures_synchronization_synchronization_policy", "label": "Synchronization policy (batch 32, uploaded inputs)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "benchmark/report/figures/synchronization.svg", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "benchmark_report_figures_syn